course: dictionary
lesson: 7210
title: Chemical messages
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Animals talk to each other too, but not always with sounds or pictures. Some send messages with chemicals. A pheromone is a chemical that an animal gives off to send a message to others of its own kind, as in «Ants leave smell trails called pheromones».
explain: Pheromones can say «follow me», «danger» or «I am looking for a mate». The message is picked up by smell or taste, not by sight or hearing. Ants smell it with their antennae.
explain: The words around pheromone give clues. The trail leads to good food, more ants follow and lay more smell, so the trail gets stronger. Check by swapping in your meaning: a chemical message that ants smell still makes sense.
fact: A pheromone is a chemical message given out by an animal.
fact: Pheromones are smelled or tasted by other animals; they are not seen or heard.
fact: A «pheromone trail» is a line of scent that other animals can follow, as ants do to food.
fact: An «alarm pheromone» is a chemical that warns others of danger, as bees give out.
fact: Farmers use «pheromone traps» to catch pests without spraying.
exercise: «Ants leave smell trails called pheromones.» What is a pheromone? | *A chemical an animal gives off to send a message to others of its kind | A picture drawn on leaves | A kind of seed that ants carry home | A loud sound that animals make to call their friends
exercise: «Ants that find food lay a scent trail of chemicals called pheromones on the way home.» What does a pheromone trail help ants do? | Hear the queen singing | See the food from far away | Fly above the ground | *Follow the way to the food
exercise: Bees give out an alarm pheromone. What does it tell other bees? | Time for a picnic | *Danger | Sing a song | Come and see the flowers
test: How do other animals pick up a pheromone? | By hearing | By reading a message on the ground | By sight | *By smelling or tasting it
test: «Moths find mates by pheromones from far away.» What does mate mean here? | *A partner of its own kind | A food store | A nest in a tree | A friend to play with
exam: Which job is a pheromone trap used for? | *Catching pests without spraying | Catching fish in a river | Warning ships of rocks | Cooking food
